{% extends "layout.njk" %}
{% import "macros.njk" as m %}
{% set title = "Dialog" %}
{% set slug = "dialog" %}

{% block content %}
<h1>Dialog</h1>
<p class="lede">
  A centered, backdrop-blurred modal built on the native <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>
  element. Open it with <code>showModal()</code>.
</p>

{{ m.classes([
  ["dialog", "The dialog itself: fixed, centered, blurred backdrop, hidden unless open"],
  ["dialog-box", "The panel inside: card surface, scrolls internally, capped width"],
  ["dialog-header", "Header row inside the panel"],
  ["dialog-actions", "Footer row inside the panel: top border, right-aligned actions"]
]) }}

<h2>Basic</h2>
{% set classless %}
<button data-dialog="demo-dialog">Open dialog</button>

<dialog id="demo-dialog">
  <form method="dialog">
    <article>
      <header>
        <h3>Dialog heading</h3>
      </header>
      <p>Scrolls internally when the content is taller than the viewport, and locks
        scrolling on the page behind it.</p>
      <footer>
        <button value="cancel" class="secondary">Cancel</button>
        <button value="ok">OK</button>
      </footer>
    </article>
  </form>
</dialog>
{% endset %}
{% set classes %}
<button class="btn" data-dialog="demo-dialog">Open dialog</button>

<dialog id="demo-dialog" class="dialog">
  <form method="dialog">
    <article class="dialog-box prose">
      <header class="dialog-header">
        <h3>Dialog heading</h3>
      </header>
      <p>Scrolls internally when the content is taller than the viewport, and locks
        scrolling on the page behind it.</p>
      <footer class="dialog-actions">
        <button value="cancel" class="btn btn-secondary">Cancel</button>
        <button value="ok" class="btn">OK</button>
      </footer>
    </article>
  </form>
</dialog>
{% endset %}
{{ m.demo("dialog-basic", classless, classes) }}
<p>
  <small>
    The docs script wires <code>[data-dialog]</code> to <code>showModal()</code>. In your own
    app that is one line: <code>dialog.showModal()</code>.
  </small>
</p>

<h2>Form nesting</h2>
<p>
  Both nestings work, so the close button needs no JavaScript:
  <code>dialog &gt; form &gt; .dialog-box</code> and
  <code>dialog &gt; .dialog-box &gt; form</code>.
</p>
{% set classless %}
<button data-dialog="inner-form-dialog">Open</button>

<dialog id="inner-form-dialog">
  <article>
    <form method="dialog">
      <header><h3>Rename</h3></header>
      <label>
        Name
        <input type="text" value="Untitled" />
      </label>
      <footer>
        <button value="cancel" class="secondary">Cancel</button>
        <button value="save">Save</button>
      </footer>
    </form>
  </article>
</dialog>
{% endset %}
{% set classes %}
<button class="btn" data-dialog="inner-form-dialog">Open</button>

<dialog id="inner-form-dialog" class="dialog">
  <article class="dialog-box prose">
    <form method="dialog">
      <header class="dialog-header"><h3>Rename</h3></header>
      <label class="label">
        Name
        <input type="text" class="input" value="Untitled" />
      </label>
      <footer class="dialog-actions">
        <button value="cancel" class="btn btn-secondary">Cancel</button>
        <button value="save" class="btn">Save</button>
      </footer>
    </form>
  </article>
</dialog>
{% endset %}
{{ m.demo("dialog-form", classless, classes) }}
{{ m.classless_note("the panel is an <code>article</code>, so it picks up the card styling automatically. With classes, <code>.dialog-box</code> includes it.") }}
{# composition #}
<h2 id="composition">Composition</h2>
<p>
  Common ways to put this together with other components. The general rules are on
  <a href="./composition.html">Composition</a>.
</p>

<h3>A form in a dialog</h3>
<p>A short form that opens over the page, with its actions at the foot of the box.</p>
{% set classless %}
<button class="btn" data-dialog="comp-dialog">Book a call</button>

<dialog id="comp-dialog" class="dialog">
  <form method="dialog">
    <article class="dialog-box">
      <header class="dialog-header prose"><h3>Book a call</h3><p class="text-secondary">Pick a time and we will confirm by email.</p></header>
      <label class="label"><p>Email</p><input class="input" type="email" /></label>
      <label class="label">
        <p>Time</p>
        <select class="select"><option>Tuesday morning</option><option>Thursday afternoon</option></select>
      </label>
      <footer class="dialog-actions">
        <button value="cancel" class="btn btn-secondary">Cancel</button>
        <button value="ok" class="btn">Book</button>
      </footer>
    </article>
  </form>
</dialog>
{% endset %}
{{ m.demo("dialog-comp-1", classless) }}
<ul>
  <li><strong>Surface:</strong> <code>.dialog-box</code> is a card, and <code>.dialog-actions</code> is <code>.card-actions</code> aligned right, so a dialog composes like any card.</li>
  <li><strong>Text:</strong> <code>.prose</code> goes on the header only; the fields space themselves.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Confirming a destructive action</h3>
<p>The dialog names what will be lost, the safe choice is secondary, and the destructive one takes the error colour.</p>
{% set classless %}
<button class="btn btn-secondary" data-dialog="comp-confirm">Delete project</button>

<dialog id="comp-confirm" class="dialog">
  <form method="dialog">
    <article class="dialog-box prose">
      <h3>Delete this project?</h3>
      <p>Its files and history go with it. This cannot be undone.</p>
      <footer class="dialog-actions">
        <button value="cancel" class="btn btn-secondary">Keep it</button>
        <button value="ok" class="btn error">Delete</button>
      </footer>
    </article>
  </form>
</dialog>
{% endset %}
{{ m.demo("dialog-comp-2", classless) }}
<ul>
  <li><strong>Boundary:</strong> <code>.error</code> works on any element, so the delete button becomes an error button with no button-specific class.</li>
  <li><strong>Actions:</strong> the button that opens it is <code>.btn-secondary</code>; a destructive action should not be the loudest thing on the page.</li>
</ul>
{% endblock %}
